Gaps\n\n## Expected vs. Observed\n| Expected Behavior | Observed Risk 
|\n|---|---|\n| If `lines_read` contains "full" as a token (e.g., `"full"`, 
`"full,10-20"`, `"10-20,full"`), the file should be considered fully read | Only
exact match `"full"` (after normalization) is recognized as "file read fully" 
|\n| Contradictory `lines_read` values like `"full,10-20"` should either be 
rejected as invalid or treated as "full" (since "full" takes precedence) | 
`"full,10-20"` is parsed as line range 10-20 only, ignoring the "full" token 
|\n| The presence of "full" should override any line range specifications | Line
ranges in `"full,10-20"` are parsed and checked, leading to incorrect "should 
read" decisions |\n\n## Root Cause\nIn `should_read_file` method lines 
410-420:\n```python\nnormalized = "".join(match.lines_read.split()).lower()\nif 
normalized == "full":\n    return False, "File already read (full)"\n```\nThe 
check is for exact equality `normalized == "full"`. For 
`lines_read="full,10-20"`, normalization produces `"full,10-20"` which doesn\'t 
equal `"full"`.\n\nThe `_parse_line_ranges` method also has issues: for 
`"full,10-20"`, it splits by comma to get `["full", "10-20"]`, tries to parse 
`"full"` as integer (fails), skips it, then parses `"10-20"` as a line range. So
it ignores the "full" token.\n\n## Fix Plan\n1. Modify the "full" check in 
`should_read_file` to check if `lines_read` contains "full" as a token (after 
splitting by comma and normalizing)\n2. Update `_parse_line_ranges` to return 
empty list (or special value) when "full" is present\n3. Consider adding 
validation to `WorkingMemoryEntry` to reject contradictory `lines_read` 
values\n\n## Status: FIXED\n\n## Files Changed\n1. 
